{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 62, :comments ["<b>And Isaac came from the approach.</b> The plain sense of the verse is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: where did he go? To bring Hagar — she who sat by the well and called it באר לחי ראי; see in my humiliation." "<b>And it is fitting</b> to say that it alludes to the attribute called כנסת ישראל, which corresponds to Mount Moriah. It says, ויצחק בא מבוא — 'and Isaac came from the approach,' that is, from כנסת ישראל; he is the well referred to as לחי עולמים, which he would see, that is, that he would flow into it. And the verse hints that while Isaac was still dwelling in the land of the Negev, his thought was roaming in כנסת ישראל, drawing from his attribute, and he came from there — and this is the language of עם באר לחי ראי, 'with the well of לחי ראי,' and it did not say בבאר, 'in the well.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {1 "The final phrase לחי עולמים (\"living eternally\") appears to replace לחי ראי of the biblical text; retained as written. The word מושכ׳ appears with a defective spelling in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:52.214406991Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}